  "result": "The 10-minute lesson on O3 is written, with a quiz and key. I haven't opened the lesson in a browser or run its practice buttons, and I didn't count its words, so the 10-minute fit is only estimated from the section plan.\n\n- **Lesson:** `lessons/0001-context-and-memory.html`\n- **What it covers:**\n  - The context window, and why the model keeps nothing from one call to the next.\n  - Memory between steps (the message list) and between sessions (notes saved to a file and reloaded).\n  - Retrieval of manual chunks, with a short code sketch.\n  - A worked token-cost example comparing a 1,500-token chunk (17,400 input tokens in total) with a 12,000-token chapter (80,400).\n- **Your turn:** a fresh version of that cost calculation to finish yourself, with the solution on demand.\n- **Sources:** Anthropic's context-engineering post is the primary source. The lesson also cites the Claude context-window docs and the \"Lost in the Middle\" paper, and all three are in `RESOURCES.md`. I found no vetted community, so the lesson points you to your design team instead.\n- **Quiz:** quiz 0002 (five items) is ready whenever you want it, with its key in `keys/0002.json`. I will not reveal answers.\n\nI also updated `NOTES.md`. It now records the new misconception M3, that the model itself remembers earlier steps or visits.\n\nQuiz 0001 is still ungraded, with submissions s1 and s2 waiting. Should I grade them next, or would you rather take quiz 0002 first?",
